# 10. DAILY MEAL LOGGING AND DIET ACCOUNTABILITY

THIS IS ONE OF THE MOST IMPORTANT FEATURES OF THE APPLICATION.

The trainer needs to know what members actually eat, not only what they are supposed to eat.

Build a detailed daily food tracking system.

### Member Meal Logging

For every scheduled meal, the member should be able to submit:

- Meal name.
- Date and time.
- Foods actually consumed.
- Quantity of each food.
- Estimated portion size.
- Whether the meal followed the assigned plan.
- Optional meal photograph.
- Optional notes.

Allow members to select foods from their assigned diet plan and record whether they followed the prescribed quantity.

If a member eats something outside the plan, provide an option to record the additional food.

The system should preserve the original assigned meal so the trainer can compare the prescribed meal with the actual meal.

### Meal Photo Evidence

Allow members to upload photographs of their meals.

Display each submitted meal photograph in the trainer's dashboard with:
- Member name.
- Member photograph.
- Meal name.
- Submission time.
- Reported foods.
- Reported quantities.
- Assigned meal comparison.
- Trainer review status.

Allow the trainer to:
- Review meal reports.
- Approve reports.
- Request corrections.
- Add feedback.
- Flag reports for follow-up.

Do not automatically claim that a photograph proves the exact ingredients, quantities, or calories. Any image-based food recognition should be treated as an estimate that the member or trainer can correct.

### Missed Meal Tracking

If a scheduled meal has not been logged by the configured reporting deadline:
- Mark the meal as Not Reported.
- Show it in the trainer's pending activity list.
- Notify the trainer when appropriate.
- Allow the trainer to request an update.

Distinguish between:
- Followed Plan.
- Partially Followed.
- Deviated from Plan.
- Skipped Meal.
- Not Reported.

A missing report must not automatically be interpreted as proof that the member skipped a meal.

### Daily Diet Compliance

Calculate a transparent daily compliance score based on configured meal requirements and submitted logs.

For example, if four equally weighted meals are scheduled and three are verified as following the plan, the system may display 75% reported meal compliance.

Clearly distinguish reported compliance from independently verified compliance.

Show the trainer:
- Daily meal compliance.
- Weekly diet compliance.
- Meals followed.
- Meals partially followed.
- Meals skipped.
- Meals not reported.
- Extra foods consumed.
- Calories and macronutrients reported by the member.

The trainer should be able to see which members are consistently following their diet plans and which members need additional support.

